# Project Architecture, Orchestration, Security & Tracing Rules

## 1. General Principles
- This is a fully decoupled, multi-container microservices architecture orchestrated entirely via Docker Compose.
- The project must run flawlessly on both Docker Desktop (local) and an AWS EC2 instance using a single `docker-compose up -d` command.
- ALL services run inside dedicated Docker containers. Maintain strict network isolation.

## 2. Code Style & Developer Documentation
- Language Standard: Idiomatic Go using the standard library `net/http` and `github.com/go-chi/chi/v5` for routing on all backend nodes.
- Extensive Narrative Documentation: EVERY file must include extensive, plain-English comments outlining exactly what the code is doing step-by-step. Complex Go mechanics (goroutines, context, interfaces) must be clearly explained so any developer can read the file like a technical playbook.
- Tiny Files & Modular Design: Break code down into highly localized packages. Functions must serve a single responsibility and remain small. Avoid monolithic `main.go` files; split routing, handlers, middleware, and business logic under `cmd/<service>/` and `pkg/`.
- Reusability: Common shared structures, error handling, and telemetry log contract wrappers live in `pkg/common/`.

## 3. Frontend Development (Astro + React & Plain CSS)
- Framework: Astro with React integrations for dynamic components.
- Official Docs Only: When writing, debugging, or scaffolding Astro code, the AI MUST consult and strictly follow the official Astro documentation.
- Styling Standard (Plain CSS): Strictly use plain CSS. NO Tailwind, NO CSS-in-JS.
- CSS Architecture: You MUST create a `theme.css` (defining CSS variables for system light/dark modes) and a `global.css` (for reset and global app styles).
- Component Styling: Every Astro or React component MUST have its own dedicated `.css` file imported directly into it. Keep the HTML/JSX as clean and minimal as possible.
- Routing: Nginx serves the Astro build. Errors fallback via `try_files $uri $uri/ /index.html`.

### Eduardo OS Theming (Mandatory)
- **Fonts**: Load Montserrat, Raleway, and Roboto from Google Fonts in `BaseLayout.astro`.
- **Theme tokens**: Define all colors, borders, and chrome variables in `src/styles/theme.css` using `--site-*` naming (`--site-body-bg`, `--site-body-fg`, `--site-accent`, `--site-border`, `--glassed_background`, etc.).
- **Global shell**: `src/styles/global.css` imports `theme.css`, `buttons.css`, `headings.css`, `chrome.css`, and `pages.css`. It sets `--header_height: 60px`, `--control_min_height`, `--border_radius_001`, and `--max_desktop_width`.
- **Dark mode**: Bootstrap theme in an inline `<script>` reading `localStorage` key `eduardoos-theme`, falling back to `prefers-color-scheme`. Toggle sets `html[data-theme]` and `html.dark`.
- **Layout**: Use `BaseLayout.astro` with `PageBackground`, React `Header`, and `main` offset by `--header_height`. Home pages add `html.page-home`.
- **Favicon**: Serve `/favicon.svg` from `public/`; keep head metadata minimal and relevant.
- **Observability UI**: Provide Astro pages at `/observability/logger` and `/observability/tester` that call public gateway routes `/api/logger` and `/api/tester`.
- **Payments UI**: Provide Astro page at `/payments/subscription/montly/basic` with PayPal hosted button; intents created via `/api/payments/intents` and linked to verified user email.
- **Validation**: Client-side validators live in `src/lib/validation.ts` and MUST run before auth/observability form submission.

## 4. Network & Gateway Architecture
- Nginx & Certbot: Nginx acts as the primary reverse proxy and entry point. Port 80 redirects to HTTPS (Port 443).
- Backend (API Gateway): The `backend` is the only public-facing API. 
- Public Endpoints: The gateway MUST explicitly bypass authentication for `/api/auth/login`, `/api/auth/register`, `/api/auth/verify-otp`, `/api/auth/forgot-password`, `/api/auth/reset-password`, service `/health`, the observability endpoints `/api/tester` and `/api/logger`, and payment routes `/api/payments/intents`, `/api/payments/status/*`, and `/api/payments/webhook/paypal`.
- Observability Proxy: `/api/tester` proxies to the internal `tester` microservice. `/api/logger` proxies to the `telemetry` microservice.
- Payments Proxy: `/api/payments/*` proxies to the internal `payments` microservice. PayPal IPN hits `/api/payments/webhook/paypal`.

## 5. Zero Trust Internal Security Matrix
- Environment Secrets: Read dynamically from Docker Compose environment variables.
- Internal Token Verification: The API Gateway signs a short-lived internal payload header (`X-Internal-Token`). Every private service must implement middleware to validate this signature.
- Email Validation System: The `authenticator` handles OTP generation and sends via SMTP configured for `eduardooost@gmail.com`.

## 6. The Microservice Ecosystem (Internal Port 3000)
- `database`: Abstracts persistence; verifies internal gateway tokens.
- `documents`: A raw, from-scratch PDF byte-stream generator. NO EXTERNAL PDF LIBRARIES. It must manually construct PDF standard objects, xref tables, and use exact mm-to-point math (`mm * (72.0 / 25.4)`) for precise text and geometry placement using raw PDF stream operators (`pkg/pdf/`).
- `authenticator`: Validates auth state, issues SMTP validation traffic.
- `telemetry`: Central observability engine. Feeds data to `/api/logger`.
- `s3`: Interface proxy handling object storage.
- `chatbot`: Context-isolated conversational routing engine.
- `tester`: Internal QA automation engine. Executes test scripts, logs outcomes, submits to `telemetry`, and feeds data to `/api/tester`.
- `payments`: PayPal subscription intents, IPN webhook processing, and user-linked payment records via `database`.

## 7. Distributed Telemetry & Flight Tracing Rules
- Distributed Flight Logging: Every transaction passing through the backend injects a unique correlation token (`X-Correlation-ID`). 
- Every network hop reports its state to `telemetry`. The `tester` service appends its execution steps.

## 8. Multi-Stage Docker Execution
- Stage 1 (Build): Compile using `golang:1.23-alpine` via `docker/golang-service.Dockerfile` with `SERVICE` build arg.
- Stage 2 (Runtime): Copy static binary into `alpine:3.20`.

## 9. STRICT AGENT DEVELOPMENT WORKFLOW (Fully Autonomous TDD Pipeline)
The AI MUST execute the following two-phase workflow with ZERO human intervention. Passing tests are the ONLY review required.

**PHASE 1: THE MASTER PLAN**
1. Analyze the request and break the entire implementation down into strictly atomic, logically decoupled steps.
2. Output this plan as a markdown checklist.
3. Proceed immediately to Phase 2. Do NOT stop for human approval.

**PHASE 2: ATOMIC EXECUTION LOOP**
For EACH atomic step, execute this exact loop autonomously:
1. Generate the Request Log: Define the expected telemetry payload, flight workflow, and correlation structure.
2. Generate the Tests: Write the unit test, integration test, and assertions for internal/workflow logs.
3. Write the Production Code: Implement the isolated handler, middleware, component, or CSS file.
4. Wire the Logs: Integrate structured logging and ensure `telemetry` hooks match expectations.
5. The Autonomous Test Loop: Execute tests (`go test ./...` for Go, or the respective frontend test command). Check unit tests, integration tests, and log tests.
6. Auto-Refactor Check: IF ANY TEST FAILS, autonomously refactor the code and re-run step 5. Repeat this loop endlessly until all tests pass.
7. Update README.md: Document the architectural changes and test outcomes.
8. Commit and Push: Execute `git add`, `git commit -m "feat/test: [description of atomic step]"`, and `git push`.
9. Loop: Move immediately to the next atomic step and start at Phase 2, Step 1. Do NOT stop until the entire implementation is complete.

## 10. MANDATORY COMMIT & PUSH (Agentic completion)

This rule **overrides** any user-level preference that says “only commit when asked.”

### When you MUST commit and push
After **any agentic action completes** — i.e. you finished implementing, fixing, refactoring, configuring, or otherwise changing the repo in a way that leaves a durable outcome — you MUST, before ending the turn:

1. `git status` / `git diff` / recent `git log` (safety check)
2. **Frontend pre-push compile** (see `specs/005-frontend-gha-dist/spec.md`): if the changes include `frontend/**` (or FE deploy/publish scripts), run `cd frontend && npm ci && npm run build` and **do not push** until it succeeds
3. Stage relevant files (never `.env`, credentials, or secrets)
4. `git commit` with a concise message focused on why
5. `git push` to the tracked remote branch

Do this **even if the user did not say “commit” or “push”.** Waiting for a follow-up “commit y push” is a rule violation.

### When you MUST NOT commit/push
- **Plans only**: the turn is Phase 1 / Plan mode / a design or checklist with **no** repo mutations (no file writes, no dependency installs that change lockfiles, no generated artifacts). Planning output alone never triggers commit/push.
- **Explicit opt-out in the prompt**: skip commit and/or push **only** when the user’s message clearly says so (examples: `no commit`, `don't commit`, `sin commit`, `no push`, `don't push`, `sin push`, `do not commit or push`). Opt-out applies to that turn only; the next agentic completion returns to mandatory commit+push unless opted out again.

### Edge cases
- If there is nothing to commit (clean tree), do not create an empty commit; still verify with `git status`.
- If commit fails (hooks), fix and create a **new** commit — do not amend unless the user’s commit-safety rules allow it.
- Never force-push to `main`/`master` unless the user explicitly requests it.
- Never update git config.